Title
Longitudinally Extensive Dorsal Column Lesion in a Patient with
Longstanding Anti-Hu and Anti-Amphphysin Autoimmunity
Presentations
P1 - Poster Session 1
Background
Anti-Hu antibodies are associated with paraneoplastic syndromes,
classically SSN.  We report a patient with a non-paraneoplastic anti-Hu SSN
who had selective dorsal column changes on spine MRI. 
Objective
To describe the clinical and unique radiological features of a patient with
Anti-Hu-mediated subacute sensory neuronopathy (SSN) and co-existing
stiff person syndrome (SPS).
Design/Methods
Case report.
Results
<seg id="s0001" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">In 2003, a 57-year-old man presented with body spasms, leg stiffness, and
dysphagia. Initial work up demonstrated elevated serum antiGAD65
antibodies, CSF pleocytosis (11 WBCs;87% monocytes) and an
unremarkable MRI of neuraxis, body CT, and electrophysiological studies.
He improved with pulse steroids. Diagnosis of SPS was made and was put
on Mycophenolate (MMF) and monthly IVIG with complete improvement.
In 2009, he developed impaired proprioception/vibration/sharp
sensation(non-length dependent pattern), gait dysfunction, and diffuse
hyporeflexia. Nerve conduction studies revealed absent sensory potentials
in multiple nerves but normal motor potentials. He had positive anti-
